meta {
  name: Create group
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{groupService}}/groups
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {}
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    bru.setEnvVar("groupKey", res.body.groupKey);
    bru.setEnvVar("groupPrefix", res.body.groupId + "/");
  }
}

tests {
  test("answers a group key and the id its streams live under", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.groupKey).to.be.a("string");
    expect(res.body.groupId).to.be.a("string");
  });
}

docs {
  # Create group

  Draws a group key and hands it back with the id its streams live under.
  Nothing is stored, because there is nothing to store: the group exists by somebody holding the key, and the id is that key's own keyed digest.

  The group key is the invite. Holding one is what lets somebody join, friends share it once, and it never expires, so a person is removed from a group by drawing a new one rather than by any button in the app.

  It is the whole secret, so the response is the only time it is readable.
  The post-response script writes it and the prefix (`groupId` plus a slash) into the environment, where every later request reads them.

  Creation is open and therefore bounded rather than gated: one address gets `CreationsPerHour` of them, and the one over is refused with 429.
  Behind a reverse proxy the bound counts the proxy, which leaves the real one to the proxy and makes this a backstop.
}
